@@host: Philadelphia. The winter of eighteen forty-two.

@@host: In a small rented house, while his young wife lies coughing blood in the next room, a man sits by lamplight and writes. He is thirty-three years old. He is poor, and proud, and very often drunk. His name is Edgar Allan Poe.

@@host: The story he writes that winter is barely two thousand words long. It has no names, no town, no date. Only a voice, speaking to us in the dark. A voice that wants, more than anything, to convince us that it is sane.

@@host: It will be published the following January, in a small magazine called The Pioneer, for the sum of ten dollars.

@@host: Listen closely. He says his hearing is very, very good.

